    <section anchor="interface">
      <name>Interface</name>
      <sourcecode type="ts-interface"><![CDATA[
// module: nish/crypto/hmac
export const hmacSha256 = (key: u8[], data: u8[]): u8[]
export const hmacSha384 = (key: u8[], data: u8[]): u8[]
export const hmacSha512 = (key: u8[], data: u8[]): u8[]
// module: nish/crypto/sha256
export const sha256 = (data: u8[]): u8[]
// module: nish/crypto/sha512
export const sha384 = (data: u8[]): u8[]
export const sha512 = (data: u8[]): u8[]
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
    <section anchor="the-functions">
      <name>The Functions</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-HMAC-SHA256">
        <t>HMAC-SHA-256 is the realization of the HMAC message authentication code using the SHA-256 hash function
<xref section="1" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Note: the properties below hold hmacSha256 to the HMAC construction of
<xref target="HMAC"/> over nish/crypto/sha256 (block length 64 bytes, ipad 0x36, opad
0x5c, a key longer than the block hashed first) and to a 32-byte tag.</t>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-CONSTRUCTION-256 covers R-HMAC-SHA256 — HMAC-SHA-256 of a key of at most 64 bytes is the RFC 2104 construction over SHA-256
forall (key: u8[] /* 0..64 */, text: u8[] /* 0..200 */) => hmacSha256(key, text) === sha256(xor(key.concat(zeros(64 - key.length)), fill(92, 64)).concat(sha256(xor(key.concat(zeros(64 - key.length)), fill(54, 64)).concat(text))))
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-LONG-KEY-256 covers R-HMAC-SHA256 — a key longer than 64 bytes gives the tag of its SHA-256 hash
forall (key: u8[] /* 65..300 */, text: u8[] /* 0..100 */) => hmacSha256(key, text) === hmacSha256(sha256(key), text)
]]></sourcecode>
      <blockquote anchor="R-HMAC-SHA384">
        <t>HMAC-SHA-384 is the realization of the HMAC message authentication code using the SHA-384 hash function
<xref section="1" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Note: as for SHA-256, with SHA-384, a block length of 128 bytes and a
48-byte tag.</t>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-CONSTRUCTION-384 covers R-HMAC-SHA384 — HMAC-SHA-384 of a key of at most 128 bytes is the RFC 2104 construction over SHA-384
forall (key: u8[] /* 0..128 */, text: u8[] /* 0..300 */) => hmacSha384(key, text) === sha384(xor(key.concat(zeros(128 - key.length)), fill(92, 128)).concat(sha384(xor(key.concat(zeros(128 - key.length)), fill(54, 128)).concat(text))))
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-LONG-KEY-384 covers R-HMAC-SHA384 — a key longer than 128 bytes gives the tag of its SHA-384 hash
forall (key: u8[] /* 129..400 */, text: u8[] /* 0..100 */) => hmacSha384(key, text) === hmacSha384(sha384(key), text)
]]></sourcecode>
      <blockquote anchor="R-HMAC-SHA512">
        <t>HMAC-SHA-512 is the realization of the HMAC message authentication code using the SHA-512 hash function
<xref section="1" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Note: as for SHA-256, with SHA-512, a block length of 128 bytes and a
64-byte tag.</t>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-CONSTRUCTION-512 covers R-HMAC-SHA512 — HMAC-SHA-512 of a key of at most 128 bytes is the RFC 2104 construction over SHA-512
forall (key: u8[] /* 0..128 */, text: u8[] /* 0..300 */) => hmacSha512(key, text) === sha512(xor(key.concat(zeros(128 - key.length)), fill(92, 128)).concat(sha512(xor(key.concat(zeros(128 - key.length)), fill(54, 128)).concat(text))))
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-LONG-KEY-512 covers R-HMAC-SHA512 — a key longer than 128 bytes gives the tag of its SHA-512 hash
forall (key: u8[] /* 129..400 */, text: u8[] /* 0..100 */) => hmacSha512(key, text) === hmacSha512(sha512(key), text)
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TAG-LEN covers R-HMAC-SHA256, R-HMAC-SHA384, R-HMAC-SHA512 — the tags are 32, 48 and 64 bytes, the hash output, for any key and message
forall (key: u8[] /* 0..200 */, text: u8[] /* 0..200 */) => hmacSha256(key, text).length === 32 && hmacSha384(key, text).length === 48 && hmacSha512(key, text).length === 64
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
    <section anchor="the-test-cases">
      <name>The Test Cases</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-SHORT-KEY">
        <t>Test with a key shorter than the length of the HMAC output.
<xref section="4.3" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-KEY-DATA-64">
        <t>Test with a combined length of key and data that is larger than 64 bytes (= block-size of SHA-224 and SHA-256).
<xref section="4.4" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-KEY-DATA-64-TC4">
        <t>Test with a combined length of key and data that is larger than 64 bytes (= block-size of SHA-224 and SHA-256).
<xref section="4.5" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Note: Sections 4.4 and 4.5 describe test cases 3 and 4 in the same
sentence; each is quoted under its own section.</t>
      <blockquote anchor="R-TRUNC">
        <t>Test with a truncation of output to 128 bits.
<xref section="4.6" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Note: the functions return the whole tag, so test case 5 (<xref section="4.6" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/>: a key of 20 bytes of 0x0c, the data "Test With Truncation") is
written as a slice of it, its first 16 bytes. This is a limit of vector: a
vector row holds a function's whole return value, so a truncated tag can
only be a property.</t>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TRUNC-256 covers R-TRUNC, R-HMAC-SHA256 — RFC 4231 test case 5: the first 128 bits of HMAC-SHA-256 are a3b6167473100ee06e0c796c2955552b
forall (n: i32 /* 16..16 */) => hmacSha256(fill(12, 20), utf8("Test With Truncation")).slice(0, n) === [a3 b6 16 74 73 10 0e e0 6e 0c 79 6c 29 55 55 2b]
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TRUNC-384 covers R-TRUNC, R-HMAC-SHA384 — RFC 4231 test case 5: the first 128 bits of HMAC-SHA-384 are 3abf34c3503b2a23a46efc619baef897
forall (n: i32 /* 16..16 */) => hmacSha384(fill(12, 20), utf8("Test With Truncation")).slice(0, n) === [3a bf 34 c3 50 3b 2a 23 a4 6e fc 61 9b ae f8 97]
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TRUNC-512 covers R-TRUNC, R-HMAC-SHA512 — RFC 4231 test case 5: the first 128 bits of HMAC-SHA-512 are 415fad6271580a531d4179bc891d87a6
forall (n: i32 /* 16..16 */) => hmacSha512(fill(12, 20), utf8("Test With Truncation")).slice(0, n) === [41 5f ad 62 71 58 0a 53 1d 41 79 bc 89 1d 87 a6]
]]></sourcecode>
      <blockquote anchor="R-KEY-128">
        <t>Test with a key larger than 128 bytes (= block-size of SHA-384 and SHA-512).
<xref section="4.7" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-KEY-DATA-128">
        <t>Test with a key and data that is larger than 128 bytes (= block-size of SHA-384 and SHA-512).
<xref section="4.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC4231"/></t>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
